{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>When your brother becomes poor and sells from his ancestral holding.</b> Our Rabbis, of blessed memory (Sukkah 29b), interpreted the entire sequence of these adjacent passages as referring to one who deals in produce of the Sabbatical year, whose end is to come to poverty. And there is a semblance of proof for this in what it says: ״וְכִי תִמְכְּרוּ מִמְכָּר״ — it doubled the language of selling to tell you that one sale drags along another: when you sell produce of the Sabbatical year, that sale dr along a sale of movable goods to your fellow, then his field, then himself, then to a ger — for each one is harsher than the one before it; see in the Yalkut (Bihar, 25, remez 631). And it appears that the change of language across all these passages points to this: for in the sale of movable goods it did not mention ״כִּי יָמוּךְ״, for it can be that sometimes a person sells his movable goods even though he is not so impoverished, only that he needs cash. But from his ancestral holding, even a part of it, he does not sell until ״כִּי יָמוּךְ״ — but still his hand has not sunk so low; therefore in the case of one who borrows at interest it adds ״כִּי יָמוּךְ אָחִיךָ וּמָטָה יָדוֹ עִמָּךְ״ — it wrote the language ״עִמָּךְ״ in connection with the sinking and not in connection with ״כִּי יָמוּךְ״, for this thing is revealed עִמָּךְ — that his hand has failed and his strength is no longer what it was; but the fact that he is מָךְ is not yet עִמָּךְ, for still his disgrace has not been revealed in the assembly, that he is impoverished. But in the case of one who sells himself, which is the harshest of them all, it says ״וְכִי יָמוּךְ אָחִיךָ עִמָּךְ״ — for already his poverty and destitution and straits are revealed עִמָּךְ, since he is compelled to sell himself. And in the case of being sold to a ger, where it is revealed also to the ger that he is impoverished, therefore it says ״וּמָךְ אָחִיךָ עִמּוֹ״. And according to this we must say that מִיכָה is a lesser state than מָטָה יָדוֹ, and both of them together are lesser than either one alone, and the ״עִמָּךְ״ of מִיכָה is lesser than the ״עִמָּךְ״ of מָטָה יָדוֹ, and the ״עִמּוֹ״ of the ger is lesser than all of them."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The word זוּזֵי (zuzin) is rendered as 'cash,' following its common Talmudic usage for coinage. The phrase 'both of them together are lesser than either one alone' translates a deliberately paradoxical formulation in the original (גרועים מאחת מהן) which appears intentionally hyper-literal; the intended sense is that the combined state of being both מך and מטה יד is worse than either condition alone."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:38:38.828343204Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}